      "a": "wer:\nRivers originating from the Himalayas, such as the Ganga and Brahmaputra, bring mineral-rich water to the Gangetic Plains, making the soil fertile for agriculture. Additionally, these rivers provide water for drinking, irrigation, transportation, and hydroelectric power to millions of people.\nQ2. Why is India called a \u2018mini-continent\u2019?\nAnswer:\nIndia is called a \u2018mini-continent\u2019 because it has a vast area and displays huge geographical diversity, including mountains, plains, deserts, plateaus, coastal regions, islands, and varied climates, soils, cultures, and biodiversity, similar to an entire continent.\nQ3. How have people in the Thar Desert adapted to water scarcity?\nAnswer:\nPeople in the Thar Desert use traditional rainwater storage structures like taanka and kunds. They reuse water for different purposes and use sand for cleaning utensils, saving precious water in their daily routines.\nQ4. What is special about Mawlynnong Village in Meghalaya?\nAnswer:\nMawlynnong Village is famous for being extremely clean, using bamboo dustbins and eco-friendly habits. It is called the \u2018cleanest village in Asia\u2019 and is known for its living root bridges crafted by the local Khasi tribes.\nQ5. What is meant by \u2018peninsular plateau\u2019?\nAnswer:\nThe peninsular plateau is the triangular landmass in central and southern India, surrounded by water on three sides. It is an old land formation bordered by the Western and Eastern Ghats and contains mineral-rich highlands."
