      "q": "4. Long Answer Questions.\nQ1. Describe the major physical divisions of India and explain their significance in shaping Indian civilisation.",
      "a": "wer:\nIndia\u2019s major physical divisions are the Himalayas, the Gangetic Plains, the Thar Desert, the Peninsular Plateau, the Islands, and the coastal regions. Each part impacts climate, water resources, agriculture, and the settlement of people. The Himalayas protect northern India and give rise to rivers, while the plains are fertile and densely populated. The plateau is rich in minerals and forests; the coastal and island regions are important for trade, fisheries, and unique ecosystems.\nThe Himalayas (natural barrier, river sources)\nThe Gangetic Plains (fertile soil, dense population)\nArid/Desert Regions (unique lifestyles, water conservation)\nPeninsular Plateau (minerals, tribes)\nIndian Coasts and Islands (trade, biodiversity)\nQ2. Explain how India\u2019s geographical features have influenced the unity and diversity of its people.\nAnswer:\nIndia\u2019s diverse geography\u2014mountains, plains, plateaus, deserts, coasts, and islands\u2014has led to a range of climates, cultures, languages, foods, and traditions. Yet these physical barriers have also enabled the growth of a shared civilisation by providing water, fostering trade and communication, and inspiring common beliefs and practices.\nMountains shaped protection and beliefs\nRivers united people through agriculture and travel\nDiversity encouraged rich culture, while unity was maintained\nQ3. Discuss the economic and ecological roles of the peninsular plateau in India.\nAnswer:\nThe peninsular plateau provides valuable minerals (like coal), supports industries, and has fertile areas for agriculture. The forests in this region are home to many tribes who follow eco-friendly lifestyles. Numerous rivers like the Godavari and Krishna originate here, supporting both farming and hydroelectric power.\nMineral wealth and industrial base\nRiver basins for water and electricity\nForests\u2014home to biodiversity and tribes"
